\nt The Micmacs have no word exactly 
corresponding to our substantive verb. To be. 
This idea is expressed by a verbal termination
joined to the noun. But they have two words
which in signification come very  near to our verb
of existence.  One,  āŭm,  is a verb of position, and
means, To be in a  place.  The other, tĕlāe, is a
verb of condition, and means,  To be in such a
state or  condition: as:  oot tĕt āŭm,  I am here;
and,  Na  tĕlāe,  and such is my condition;  I am  so.
The verb  āŭm, which comes the nearest in
meaning to the English  verb of  existence, is
irregular in its formation and  conjugation, as in
latin, Greek and other  languages.
\nt The syllable  aam  suffixed to any  noun changes it
into a verb, and denotes  occupation-present
tense. Thus, Tŭmeegŭn,  an axe,  Tŭmeegŭnaam, 
I have an axe in  my possession.  Wĕnjeegwom,  A
house.  Wĕnjeegwŏm'aam,    I occupy a house 
